# Water at its HF/aug-cc-pVQZ optimized geometry (DALTON .OPTIMIZE, 2026-09-09,
# E = -76.066676264 Eh) -- the nightly response case. One rung (protocol 1e-4,
# k 6): alpha(0) xyz, one Raman component (d alpha / d z_O: raman.nuc_atom 0,
# raman.nuc_axis 2), two excited states (RPA) and their two-photon absorption.
# This is the geometry every DALTON comparison in the response benchmarks uses
# (refs/madness_results.json::h2o_hfopt), so the numbers can be read against
# those to about a percent; the assertions compare only to reference/.
#
# dconv 1e-3 (not 1e-4): the FD gate is bsh < 5*dconv ABSOLUTE, and the
# nuclear-displacement leg's response is ~1e3 larger than a dipole leg's, so at
# 5e-4 it plateaus near 4e-3 and Raman is dropped. 5e-3 is the loosest gate that
# still converges every leg here; the number is a regression baseline, not a
# converged Raman intensity (that lives at 1e-6/k8).
#
# Run:   ./run.sh          (or: madqc --wf=response response_h2o_raman_tpa.in)

dft
    xc        hf
    dconv     1e-4
    k         6
    protocol  [1e-4]
    l         200.0
    localize  new
    maxiter   30
end

response
    dipole                true
    dipole.directions     xyz
    dipole.frequencies    [0.0]
    requested_properties  [polarizability, raman]
    raman.nuc_atom        0
    raman.nuc_axis        2
    excited.enable        true
    excited.num_states    2
    excited.tda           false
    excited.tpa           true
    excited.maxiter       30
    dconv                 1e-3
    maxiter               60
    print_level           1
end

molecule
    eprec      1e-6
    units      atomic
    no_orient  true
    O   0.0000000000   0.0000005943   0.1925923500
    H   0.0000000000   1.4214240411  -0.8721230117
    H   0.0000000000  -1.4214246355  -0.8721234678
end
